          %h2#Idea Idea
          %p
            Deformation theory studies problems of extending structures to extensions of their domains.
            = succeed "," do
              %strong Formal deformation theory
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimal+object"} infinitesimal
          %p A typical problem in formal deformation theory has the structure that
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphism"} morphism
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo :
                      %mi X
                      %mo →
                      %mi Y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f : X \to Y
                of certain
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space"} space
            %li
              %p
                and
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimal+object"} infinitesimal
                thickenings
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mover
                        %mi X
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tilde X
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mover
                        %mi Y
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tilde Y
                of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
                are prescribed, with injection morphisms
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %mo →
                      %mover
                        %mi X
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \to \tilde X
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Y
                      %mo →
                      %mover
                        %mi Y
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y \to \tilde Y
          %p
            and asks whether a bottom horizontal morphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tilde f
            in the diagram
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi X
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mi f
                        %mtd
                          %mi Y
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mi X
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mover
                              %mi f
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mi Y
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  X &amp;\stackrel{f}{\to}&amp; Y
                  \\\
                  \\downarrow &amp;&amp; \downarrow
                  \\\
                  \\tilde X &amp;\stackrel{\tilde f}{\to}&amp; \tilde Y
                  }
          %p
            may be found. This morphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tilde f
            would be called an
            %em infinitesimal deformation
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
          %p In other words:
          .standout
            %p
              Formal deformation theory studies the
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/obstruction"} obstruction theory
              of extensions to
              %strong infinitesimal
              thickenings.
          %p
            A typical example of an
            %em infinitesimal thickening
            is a
            %em square-0-extension
            of a
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ring"} ring
          %p
            let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            be a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ring"} ring
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space"} space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            in the above diagram. Let furthermore
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
            be an
            = succeed "-module," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            = succeed "-module" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/section"} section
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+bundle"} vector bundle
            over
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %p
            Then consider the new ring, whose underlying group is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/direct+sum"} direct sum
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                    %mo ⊕
                    %mi N
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R \oplus N
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi r
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi n
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi r
                    %mn 2
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi n
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi r
                    %mn 1
                  %msub
                    %mi r
                    %mn 2
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi r
                    %mn 1
                  %msub
                    %mi n
                    %mn 2
                  %mo +
                  %msub
                    %mi n
                    %mn 1
                  %msub
                    %mi r
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  (r_1, n_1) \cdot (r_2, n_2) = (r_1 r_2, r_1 n_2 + n_1 r_2)
                  \\,.
          %p
            This is the
            %strong square 0-extension
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            by
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi N
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi N
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
            are thought of as functions with values in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimal+object"} infinitesimal quantities
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi n
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ⋅
                    %msub
                      %mi n
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n_1 \cdot n_2
          %p
            So the ring
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                  %mo ⊕
                  %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R \oplus N
            may be thought of as the ring of functions on the infinitesimal extension
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tilde X
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            all the
            %em vectors of infinitesimal length
            in the vector bundle over
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %p
            There is a canonical ring homomorphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                  %mo ⊕
                  %mi N
                  %mo →
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R\oplus N \to R
            that is the identity on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
            on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi N
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mover
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \to \tilde X
            (which in turn may be thought of as the 0-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/section"} section
            of the vector bundle on
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %p
            Similarly, let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi R
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R_2
            be another ring with module
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi N
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N_2
            and square-0 extension
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi R
                      %mn 2
                    %mo ⊕
                    %msub
                      %mi N
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R_2 \oplus N_2
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            and the ring of functions on the space of infinitesimal vectors of this vector bundle.
          %p
            In terms of these function rings, a morphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f : X \to Y
            of spaces corresponds to a ring homomorphism
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi R
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ←
                    %msub
                      %mi R
                      %mn 2
                    %mo :
                    %msup
                      %mi f
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R_1 \leftarrow R_2 : f^*
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi R
                            %mn 1
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo ←
                            %mrow
                              %msup
                                %mi f
                                %mo *
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi R
                            %mn 2
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↑
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↑
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi R
                            %mn 1
                          %mo ⊕
                          %msub
                            %mi N
                            %mn 1
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi R
                            %mn 2
                          %mo ⊕
                          %msub
                            %mi N
                            %mn 2
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  R_1 &amp;\stackrel{f^*}{\leftarrow}&amp; R_2
                  \\\
                  \\uparrow &amp;&amp; \uparrow
                  \\\
                  R_1 \oplus N_1 &amp;&amp; R_2 \oplus N_2
                  }
                  \\,.
          %p
            The obvious obstruction problem now is whether we can
            %strong deform
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi f
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^*
            to a morphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi R
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ⊕
                  %msub
                    %mi N
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ←
                  %msub
                    %mi R
                    %mn 2
                  %mo ⊕
                  %msub
                    %mi N
                    %mn 2
                  %mo :
                  %msup
                    %mover
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R_1 \oplus N_1 \leftarrow R_2 \oplus N_2 : \tilde f^*
            of rings, such that we get a commuting diagram
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi R
                            %mn 1
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo ←
                            %mrow
                              %msup
                                %mi f
                                %mo *
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi R
                            %mn 2
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↑
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↑
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi R
                            %mn 1
                          %mo ⊕
                          %msub
                            %mi N
                            %mn 1
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo ←
                            %mrow
                              %msup
                                %mover
                                  %mi f
                                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                                %mo *
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi R
                            %mn 2
                          %mo ⊕
                          %msub
                            %mi N
                            %mn 2
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  R_1 &amp;\stackrel{f^*}{\leftarrow}&amp; R_2
                  \\\
                  \\uparrow &amp;&amp; \uparrow
                  \\\
                  R_1 \oplus N_1 &amp;\stackrel{\tilde f^*}{\leftarrow}&amp; R_2 \oplus N_2
                  }
                  \\,.
          %p
            The obstruction to the existence of such lifts is measured by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology"} cohomology
            with coefficients in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cotangent+complex"} cotangent complex
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi R
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R_1
          %p
            This is the archetypical problem that deformation theory deals with. As always, after studying this a bit it turns out that in order to obtain a good theory, one needs to adopt the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nPOV"} nPOV
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ring"} Ring
            of rings, but they may have good answers only in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vertical+categorification"} categorifications
            of this for instance to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} (∞,1)-category
            of
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/E-%E2%88%9E-ring"} E-∞-ring
          %h3#Modules Modules, derivations and Kähler differentials
          %p
            In order to better see the structure of the above archetypical problem of deformation theory, we describe some aspects of the canonical
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bifibration"} bifibration
            of ring modules in a way that nicely organizes all the concepts
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/module"} module
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derivation"} derivation
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K%C3%A4hler+differential"} Kähler differential
            in a single picture that lends itself to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vertical+categorification"} vertical categorification
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Deformation+Theory"} DefTheory
          %p
            With
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ring"} Ring
            denoting the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            of (commutative, unital)
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rings"} rings
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                  %mo :
                  %mi Mod
                  %mo →
                  %mi Ring
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  p : Mod \to Ring
          %p
            for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bifibration"} bifibration
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modules"} modules
            over
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rings"} rings
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Mod
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Mod
            are pairs consisting of a ring
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            an an
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/module"} module
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi N
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi R
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi N
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi R
                    %mn 2
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi N
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (R_1,N_1) \to (R_2, N_2)
            are pairs consisting of a ring homomorphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %msub
                    %mi R
                    %mn 1
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi R
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f : R_1 \to R_2
            and a morphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo :
                  %msub
                    %mi N
                    %mn 1
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi N
                    %mn 2
                  %msub
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi f
                  %msub
                    %mi R
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F : N_1 \to N_2 \otimes_f R_2
            of
            = succeed "-modules." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi R
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R_2
          %p
            (Recall for instance from the discussion at
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sweedler+coring"} Sweedler coring
            that this bifibration is a way to think of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stack"} stack
            of algebraic
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+bundles"} vector bundles
          %p
            But there is also another functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                  %mo :
                  %mi Mod
                  %mo →
                  %mi Ring
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G : Mod \to Ring
            of interest: for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
            any
            = succeed "-module," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi N
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %mo =
                  %mi R
                  %mo ⊕
                  %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G(N) := R \oplus N
            called the
            %strong square 0-extension
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi r
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi n
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi r
                    %mn 2
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi n
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi r
                    %mn 1
                  %msub
                    %mi r
                    %mn 2
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi n
                    %mn 1
                  %msub
                    %mi r
                    %mn 2
                  %mo +
                  %msub
                    %mi n
                    %mn 2
                  %msub
                    %mi r
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  (r_1,n_1) \cdot (r_2, n_2) := (r_1 r_2, n_1 r_2 + n_2 r_1)
                  \\,.
          %p
            Moreover, there is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+transformation"} natural
            morphism of rings
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi N
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G(N) \to R
            given by sending
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi r
                    %mo ,
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ↦
                    %mi r
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (r,n) \mapsto r
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/section"} section
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi v
                  %mo :
                  %mi R
                  %mo →
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} v : R \to G(n)
            of this morphism is precisely a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derivation"} derivation
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            with values in the module
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi N
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
          %p This may be organized into a single functor
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Mod
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi I
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Ring
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  Mod \to [I,Ring]
          %p
            into the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/arrow+category"} arrow category
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ring"} Ring
            = succeed "-module" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
            to the morphism
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi N
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G(N) \to R
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi Mod
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mi p
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mi Ring
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ↘
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mo ↗
                            %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                              %mrow
                                %msub
                                  %mi d
                                  %mn 1
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                          %mi I
                          %mo ,
                          %mi Ring
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  Mod &amp;&amp;\stackrel{p}{\to}&amp;&amp; Ring
                  \\\
                  \&amp; \searrow &amp;&amp; \nearrow_{\mathrlap{d_1}}
                  \\\
                  \&amp;&amp; [I,Ring]
                  }
                  \\,.
          %p
            Finally notice that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            has a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+adjoint"} left
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+functor"} adjoint functor
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ω
                  %mo :
                  %mi Ring
                  %mo →
                  %mi Mod
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\Omega : Ring \to Mod
          %p
            that sends a ring
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            to the
            = succeed "-module" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Ω
                    %mi K
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi R
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega_K(R)
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K%C3%A4hler+differentials"} Kähler differentials
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cotangent+bundle"} cotangent bundle
          %h3#cotangent_complex Cotangent complex
          %p
            Using the module of Kähler differentials is not appropriate in general; instead we need to take its derived version. To talk about the nonabelian derived functors, Quillen introduced a model category structure on the category of simplicial commutative rings. Given a morphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f: A\to B
            of rings, which makes
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            an
            = succeed "-algebra," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi AbGr
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo −
                  %mi Alg
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} AbGr(A-Alg/B)
            of abelian group objects in the slice category
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Alg
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Alg/B
            of
            = succeed "-algebras" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            is equivalent both to the category of
            = succeed "-modules" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            by
            = succeed "-modules." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi AbGr
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo −
                  %mi Alg
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi A
                  %mo −
                  %mi Alg
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} AbGr(A-Alg/B)\to A-Alg/B
            which has a left adjoint
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Ab
                      %mrow
                        %mi B
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                        %mi A
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                    %mo −
                    %mi Alg
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi B
                    %mo →
                    %mi AbGr
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo −
                    %mi Alg
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≅
                    %msub
                      %mrow
                      %mi B
                    %mi Mod
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ab_{B/A} : A-Alg/B\to AbGr(A-Alg/B)\cong {}_B Mod
            %strong relative cotangent complex
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi L
                    %mrow
                      %mi B
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_{B/A}
            is the value on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            of the left
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+functor"} derived functor
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝕃
                    %msub
                      %mi Ab
                      %mrow
                        %mi B
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                        %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{L} Ab_{B/A}(B)
          %p
            The above situation generalizes from the category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ring"} Ring
            to an arbitrary presentable
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} (∞,1)-category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            by replacing the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bifibration"} bifibration
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Mod
                  %mo →
                  %mi Ring
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Mod \to Ring
            by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stabilization"} stabilization
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi T
                    %mi C
                  %mo →
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_C \to C
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/codomain+fibration"} codomain fibration
            of
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tangent+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} tangent (∞,1)-category
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
          %p
            The projection
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                  %mo :
                  %msub
                    %mi T
                    %mi C
                  %mo →
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p : T_C \to C
            still has a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+adjoint"} left adjoint
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ω
                  %mo :
                  %mi C
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi T
                    %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\Omega : C \to T_C
          %p
            for which a representative which is also a section (in a strict sense) of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            may be taken; any such representative is called the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cotangent+complex"} cotangent complex
            functor for
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mi Ω
                  %msub
                    %mi T
                    %mi C
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mi p
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  C \stackrel{\Omega}{\to} T_C \stackrel{p}{\to} C
          %p
            is the identity
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-functor"} (∞,1)-functor
          %h3#further_categorification Further categorification
          %p …
          %h2#deformation_theory_via_differential_graded_lie_algebras Deformation theory via differential graded Lie algebras
          %p
            Over a field of characteristic zero, there is an approach to deformation theory via
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+graded+Lie+algebras"} differential graded Lie algebras
            (or more generally
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/L-infinity+algebras"} L-infinity algebras
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "http://mathoverflow.net/questions/385/deformation-theory-and-differential-graded-lie-algebras"} def theory and dgla-s
          %p
            In this approach, one begins with an object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            (for example a scheme, or a complex manifold, or a vector bundle, or an associative algebra, or a dg category, or …) that one would like to deform. Then the general principle is that there exists a dgLa
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi L
                    %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_X
            with the property that the functor
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Def
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi L
                          %mi X
                    %mo :
                    %mi Art
                    %mo →
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Def_{L_X} : Art \to Set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo ,
                  %mi m
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (A,m)
            to the set of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Maurer-Cartan+equation"} Maurer-Cartan solutions
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi L
                    %mi X
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi m
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (L_X \otimes m)^1
            modulo the gauge action of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi L
                      %mi X
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi m
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (L_X \otimes m)^0
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo ,
                  %mi m
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (A,m)
            to the set of isomorphism classes of deformations of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            over
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Spec
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \operatorname{Spec} A
          %p
            In the case of a compact complex manifold, the dgLa in question is given by the so-called
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kodaira-Spencer+theory"} Kodaira-Spencer
            dgLa: holomorphic vector fields tensor
            = succeed "-forms" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mi q
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (0,q)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dolbeault+cohomology"} Dolbeault resolution
            of the sheaf of holomorphic vector fields). In the case of an associative algebra (or a dg algebra, or an A-infinity algebra, or a dg category, or an A-infinity category), the appropriate dgLa is the Hochschild complex with the Hochschild differential and the Gerstenhaber bracket.
          %p
            In this language, the
            %span.newWikiWord
              Tian-Todorov theorem
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Tian-Todorov+theorem"} ?
            on the unobstructedness of deformations of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Calabi-Yau+manifold"} Calabi-Yau manifold
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kodaira-Spencer+theory"} Kodaira-Spencer
            dgLa of a Calabi-Yau manifold is homotopy abelian — that is, it is quasi-isomorphic to an abelian dg Lie algebra. Barannikov-Kontsevich proved more generally that the dgLa given by holomorphic polyvector fields tensor
            = succeed "-forms" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mi q
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (0,q)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi A
                    %mn ∞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_\infty
            deformations of (a dg enhancement of) the derived category of coherent sheaves on the Calabi-Yau.
          %p The following paper is a good introduction to these ideas:
          %ul
            %li
              Marco Manetti,
              %em Deformation theory via differential graded Lie algebras
              , °(
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math/0507284"}> arXiv:0507284
              )
          %p The Kontsevich and Soibelman references below are also good.
